.TH LESS 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
less \- show text a screen at a time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B less
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B less
shows
.I file
(or, with no file or with
.BR \- ,
what comes on standard input) one screen at a time. It draws on the
terminal's alternate screen, with the tty in raw mode, and reads its keys
from
.IR /dev/tty ,
so the text can come down a pipe. The whole input is read before anything
is shown.
.PP
Long lines wrap at the terminal's width; a tab goes to the next multiple
of 8 columns, and other control characters show as
.BR ^X .
Rows past the end show as
.BR ~ .
The last row is the prompt: the file's name on the first screen,
.B (END)
on the last, else
.BR : .
.PP
When standard output is not a terminal,
.B less
copies the text to it, as
.BR cat (1)
does. With no file named and standard input a terminal, it says
"Missing filename" and exits 1.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B q Q
Quit.
.TP
.B Space f ^F ^V PageDown
Forward one screen.
.TP
.B b ^B PageUp
Back one screen.
.TP
.B Enter j e ^N Down
Forward one line.
.TP
.B k y ^P Up
Back one line.
.TP
.B d ^D
Forward half a screen.
.TP
.B u ^U
Back half a screen.
.TP
.B g < Home
Go to the start.
.TP
.B G > End
Go to the end.
.TP
.BI / text
Go to the next line containing
.I text
(a plain string, not a pattern), typed at the prompt and ended by Enter.
Backspace edits it; Escape, or Backspace on an empty line, cancels. An
empty
.I text
repeats the last search.
.TP
.B n
Repeat the last search forward.
.TP
.B N
Repeat the last search backward.
.TP
.B ^L r
Draw the screen again.
.PP
Other keys are ignored. ^C ends
.BR less ,
restoring the screen and the tty, with status 130.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if the file cannot be opened or no input was given;
130 if ended by ^C.
.SH NOTES
There are no options; a word starting with
.B \-
other than
.B \-
itself is taken as a file name. The terminal's size is read once, at
start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR man (1)
